lem  there he said I'll be right there and he  promptly hung up and he got up and he  put his jacket on I'm like Larry what  are you doing he's like oh I have to go  over to the registrar's office they had  a problem with the point-of-sale system  I'm like oh yeah small talk you know it  sucks whatever go back to my Java then  he comes back and what he's got  basically is a floppy disk  neither floppy or USB key I can't even  remember now where we were in the cycle  but he's basically got portable media  with him and I'm like what's that it's  not like a dump of the log file he's  like no no no no this is the crashed  program  I'm sorry Larry what this is the crash  program here let me show you  he slides this thing into his machine he  brings it up what I discover in that day  is that small talk is not just a  language it's the browser it's the  environment the browser and the  environment are the IDE and so when  you're running this small talk  application in the browser not the web  browser but in the small talk browser  when an exception occurs the user has  the opportunity to hibernate literally  take the entire small talk browser  program and serialize it to disk error  state and all  copy that file off to a portable media  bring it back to his desk bring it up  and he is not looking at log files he is  not attempting to piece together a stack  trace he is looking at the actual  running program at the time the error  occurred  that's just [ __ ] cool and neither the  JVM nor the CLR have even begun to  embrace that although they very well  could there are still lessons to be  learned from the languages of our  ancestors and it's worthwhile to study  them  the other thing I will point out is even  if you have never built a programming  language anywhere in your history if you  were not a computer science student  which I was not my degree is actually in  international relations you really want  to have fun buy me a beer actually glass  of wine and ask me why wa